# GitHub Copilot CLI → OTLP collector.
#
# Verified against docs.github.com/en/copilot/reference/copilot-cli-reference
# and the CLI changelog on 2026-09-11. CLI runtime re-read 2026-09-21.

# Not strictly required: setting OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT auto-enables OTel.
# Set it anyway so the intent is legible.
#
# That auto-enable is NOT a CLI convenience — it is the documented behaviour of
# the VS Code extension too, verified in the shipped 0.60.0 bundle. Anyone who
# exports OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT for another harness turns Copilot on with
# it. See README trap 9.
COPILOT_OTEL_ENABLED=true

# --- THE http:// REFUSAL IS NARROWER THAN IT LOOKS -----------------------
#
# The CLI does NOT disable export over http://, despite the widely-cited issue
# saying so. What it refuses is to attach ENTERPRISE MANAGED HEADERS to a
# cleartext or user-controlled endpoint:
#
#   "Managed OTLP headers are configured but the managed telemetry endpoint
#    '...' is not https; refusing to transmit the (sensitive) managed headers
#    over cleartext."
#
# Read from the newest runtime on this machine — @github/copilot 1.0.73, which
# ships inside the VS Code extension, not under ~/.copilot/pkg (1.0.54 there).
#
# Export to http://localhost:4318 proceeds. With no managed telemetry, nothing
# changes for you. With managed telemetry, spans arrive UNAUTHENTICATED rather
# than not at all — which looks like a collector auth problem, not a client one.
#
# Tracked: github/copilot-cli#4567 (open as of 2026-09-12). The issue's own
# framing — export dropped — was NOT reproduced in any local runtime.
#
# For an https collector you terminate yourself, the runtime supports a private
# CA and mTLS directly; no proxy needed:
#
#   OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_CERTIFICATE=/path/to/ca.pem
#   OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_CLIENT_CERTIFICATE=/path/to/client.pem
#   OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_CLIENT_KEY=/path/to/client.key
#
# The file exporter remains selected below because the CLI's own output format
# has not been verified here, not because http:// is unusable.
#
# > CAUTION: the VS Code EXTENSION's file exporter writes the literal string {}
# > for every span — README trap 8. The CLI is a different runtime with a
# > different implementation. Check with `grep -c '^{}$'` before trusting a dump.
COPILOT_OTEL_EXPORTER_TYPE=file
COPILOT_OTEL_FILE_EXPORTER_PATH=/private/tmp/copilot-otel/spans.jsonl

# Only otlp-http or file. There is no grpc on this surface — if you configure
# otlp-grpc in VS Code, the CLI runtime still uses HTTP.
#
# COPILOT_OTEL_EXPORTER_TYPE=otlp-http
# OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT=https://collector.internal:4318

# Defaults to http/json. http/protobuf needs v1.0.61 or newer; on older builds
# this variable is silently ignored (github/copilot-cli#2934).
OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_PROTOCOL=http/protobuf

OTEL_SERVICE_NAME=github-copilot

# Default false. On the VS Code side this gate is not reliably enforced —
# three open defects export content regardless.
OTEL_INSTRUMENTATION_GENAI_CAPTURE_MESSAGE_CONTENT=false

# Auth to the collector. Keep real tokens out of this file; `just leaks` scans
# *.env precisely because this is where one would end up.
# OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_HEADERS=Authorization=Bearer REPLACE_ME
